  "response": "Mechanistic disputes of this kind turn on a single structural question about the potential energy surface. A concerted mechanism passes through a single transition state connecting reactants directly to products, whereas a stepwise mechanism contains a local energy minimum corresponding to a discrete intermediate. Deciding between the two therefore requires experiments whose outcome depends on whether that minimum is ever occupied.\n\nThe most informative observations are usually stereochemical. In the cycloaddition of a cis- or trans-disubstituted alkene, the relative configuration of the substituents in the starting alkene is preserved in the cycloadduct; in other words, the product retains the stereochemistry present in the alkene substrate. Because passage from reactants to products across one transition state leaves no interval in which the substrate geometry can relax, that single-transition-state description makes the stereochemical result of such an experiment diagnostic of the pathway. What indicates that no discrete intermediate lies between reactants and products on the reaction coordinate is precisely the evidential fact that stereospecific formation of a single diastereomer supports a concerted bond-forming process.\n\nThat conclusion has exactly one rival, and the two cannot both hold while one of them must: either the reaction coordinate is free of any such species, or at least one discrete intermediate lies between reactants and products on the reaction coordinate. What decides for the second case is loss of stereochemical integrity, since scrambling of substituent stereochemistry in the product is consistent with an intermediate whose bonds can rotate before closure, and the observation of such scrambling indicates that an intermediate does intervene. At least one of the following holds, perhaps both: a diradical species exists as an energy minimum along the reaction coordinate, and a zwitterionic species exists as an energy minimum along the reaction coordinate. The characterization of the diradical as a genuine well on the surface confirms that an intermediate is interposed between reactants and products.\n\nKinetic and isotopic measurements supply complementary information about the geometry of bond formation. Secondary deuterium kinetic isotope effects measured at both reacting termini probe the synchronicity of the two bond-forming events, and such determinations are now routine. A concerted mechanism requires the two new bonds to be formed to exactly equal extents at the transition state. A further class of experiments addresses whether the reacting partners remain bound to one another throughout the transformation. Crossover experiments with isotopically or substituent-labelled partners test whether fragments separate during the transformation, and such designs are straightforward to execute. The absence of crossover products demonstrates the involvement of a freely diffusing long-lived intermediate.\n\nA dispute of this sort is ultimately settled not by any single measurement but by convergence: one mechanistic description must accommodate the stereochemical outcome, the magnitude and distribution of isotope effects, the labelling results, the solvent and substituent dependences, and the computed energy surface, while its rival is driven to increasingly contrived auxiliary suppositions. When high-level calculations locate, or fail to locate, the stationary point that the stepwise account demands, and when direct spectroscopic or ultrafast detection either captures the putative intermediate or places an upper bound on its lifetime shorter than a vibrational period, the question ceases to be open and the community adopts the surviving description.",
